@node Overview
@chapter Overview

@cindex overview
Vernier (@command{vnr}) changes the resolution, refresh rate, position,
scale, rotation, mirroring and adaptive sync of the displays connected
to a Linux session.  The same model and the same backends serve both
front ends:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@cindex editor
a graphical @dfn{editor}, where outputs appear as rectangles you drag
into place (@pxref{The Editor});

@item
@cindex command line
a @dfn{command line} that does everything the editor does and prints
tables on a terminal or JSON when piped (@pxref{Command Line}).
@end itemize

@cindex confirm or revert
@cindex revert
Vernier is built so that no change can leave you without a working
screen.  Where the display server can test a configuration, Vernier
tests it before applying it.  After every interactive change Vernier
asks whether to keep it, and if nobody answers within 15 seconds it
restores the previous configuration.  A layout that blanks every screen
therefore undoes itself.

@cindex protocols
Vernier speaks each display protocol directly.  It never runs
@command{xrandr}, @command{wlr-randr} or another program and parses its
output, so unusual mode names cannot confuse it.

@node Installation
@chapter Installation

@cindex installation
@cindex packages
Vernier ships package definitions for three package managers in the
@file{packaging} directory of its source tree:

@table @file
@item packaging/default.nix
Nix and NixOS, including Steelbore OS Bravais.  From a flake:
@example
vernier = pkgs.callPackage ./packaging/default.nix @{ @};
@end example

@item packaging/guix.scm
GNU Guix.  From the source tree:
@example
guix build -f packaging/guix.scm
@end example

@item packaging/PKGBUILD
Arch Linux and derivatives.  From the @file{packaging} directory:
@example
makepkg -si
@end example
@end table

Each package installs the @command{vnr} binary and this manual as an
Info file.

@cindex building from source
To build from source you need Rust 1.90 or later:

@example
cargo build --release --locked
@end example

@cindex run-time libraries
The binary links only the C runtime.  At run time the editor needs
@file{libxkbcommon} and, on Wayland, @file{libwayland-client} or, on X11,
@file{libX11}, @file{libX11-xcb}, @file{libXcursor} and @file{libXi}; it uses a Vulkan or OpenGL driver when one is present and
falls back to a software renderer otherwise.  The command line needs none
of them.  If a required library cannot be loaded, @command{vnr gui} names
it and exits with status 17 (@code{LIBRARY_MISSING}).

On NixOS the libraries are not on the default search path, so a plain
@command{cargo build} cannot open the editor.  Build the Nix package
instead; it sets the library path for you:

@example
nix-build -E 'with import <nixpkgs> @{@}; callPackage ./packaging/default.nix @{@}'
./result/bin/vnr gui
@end example

@node The Editor
@chapter The Editor

@cindex editor
Run @command{vnr} with no command, or @command{vnr gui}, to open the
editor.  @command{vnr gui --demo} opens it on a built-in sample layout
and never touches a real display, which is a safe way to try it.

@menu
* Arranging::           The canvas and the side panel.
* Applying::            Apply, keep and revert.
* Editor Keys::         Default key bindings.
* Screen Labels::       Output names on each physical screen.
* Screen Contents::     What each screen shows, inside its rectangle.
@end menu

@node Arranging
@section Arranging outputs

@cindex canvas
@cindex snapping
The canvas shows every output as a rectangle at its position and
logical size.  Drag a rectangle to move the output; its edges snap to
neighbouring edges within 12 screen pixels.  Hold @key{Shift} while
dragging to place it freely.

@cindex side panel
The side panel lists the outputs.  For the selected output it sets the
enabled state, mode, scale, rotation, position, mirror source, adaptive
sync and the primary flag.  Controls the backend cannot apply are
hidden.  Overlaps and outputs that touch no other output are reported in
words under the canvas; they are warnings and do not block applying.

@node Applying
@section Apply, keep and revert

@cindex apply
@cindex keep
Press @samp{Apply} to send the edited layout.  A bar then counts down
from 15 seconds with @samp{Keep} and @samp{Revert} buttons.  Keep
confirms the change; Revert, or letting the countdown reach zero,
restores the layout from before Apply.

@cindex save for niri
On wlroots compositors such as niri, a change lasts until the compositor
restarts.  The @samp{Save for niri} button writes the applied layout to
@file{~/.config/niri/vernier.kdl}; @pxref{Making Changes Permanent}.

@cindex save for GNOME
On GNOME, a change lasts until you log out.  The @samp{Save for GNOME}
button asks GNOME to save the applied layout; GNOME shows its own
@samp{Keep Changes} dialog, and saves only if you choose it within 20
seconds.

@node Editor Keys
@section Default key bindings

@cindex key bindings
Every action has a CUA binding and a Vim binding.  All of them can be
changed in the configuration file (@pxref{Configuration File}); the
action names below are the names the file uses.

@multitable @columnfractions .34 .22 .24 .20
@headitem Action @tab Name @tab CUA @tab Vim
@item Move the selected output 1 px (10 with @key{Shift})
@tab @code{left} @code{right} @code{up} @code{down}
@tab Arrow keys @tab @kbd{h} @kbd{l} @kbd{k} @kbd{j}
@item Select the next or previous output
@tab @code{next}, @code{previous}
@tab @key{PageDown}, @key{PageUp} @tab @kbd{]}, @kbd{[}
@item Apply @tab @code{apply} @tab @kbd{C-@key{RET}}, @kbd{C-s} @tab
@item Keep or revert a pending change
@tab @code{keep}, @code{revert} @tab buttons @tab @kbd{y}, @kbd{n}
@item Discard edits, or revert a pending change
@tab @code{discard} @tab @key{ESC} @tab
@item Reload from the display server
@tab @code{reload} @tab @key{F5}, @kbd{C-r} @tab
@item Show or hide screen contents
@tab @code{previews} @tab @kbd{C-p} @tab @kbd{p}
@end multitable

@key{TAB} moves keyboard focus between controls and cannot be bound.
Plain letters and arrows act only when no text field has the focus, so
typing a position never moves an output.

@node Screen Labels
@section Screen labels

@cindex screen labels
@cindex overlays
While the editor is open, each physical screen shows a small label in
its top-left corner with its output name, make and model, so you can
tell which rectangle is which screen.  The label of the selected output
is outlined.  Labels never take a click or a key.

Labels need the layer-shell protocol, which niri, sway, COSMIC and KDE
Plasma offer.  GNOME and X11 sessions get no labels.  Set
@code{overlays = false} in the configuration file to turn them off.

@node Screen Contents
@section Screen contents

@cindex screen contents
@cindex previews
Tick @samp{Show screen contents} in the editor's bottom bar, or press
@kbd{C-p}, to draw what each screen shows inside its rectangle, as
wdisplays does.  Each picture is refreshed at most once a second, and
only when the screen has changed, so a still screen costs nothing.
Pictures keep the screen's current shape; a rectangle edited to another
shape shows the picture centred in it until the change is applied.

Screen contents are off by default, because the pictures show whatever
is on screen.  Set @code{previews = true} in the configuration file to
start the editor with them on.  They are never shown in the demo
(@samp{vnr gui --demo}).

Screen contents use the @code{ext-image-copy-capture-v1} protocol where
the compositor offers it (sway 1.11 and later, COSMIC), and
@code{wlr-screencopy-unstable-v1} otherwise (niri, Hyprland, older
sway).  Where neither is offered, as on GNOME, KDE Plasma and X11
sessions, the switch turns itself off and the status line says why.

@node Command Line
@chapter Command Line

@cindex grammar
Commands follow @samp{vnr @var{noun} @var{verb}}.  On a terminal
@command{vnr} prints tables and asks before keeping a change.  Piped, in
CI, or under a coding agent it prints JSON and keeps changes without
asking.

@menu
* Global Options::      Options every command accepts.
* Output Commands::     List, inspect and change outputs.
* Other Commands::      Profiles, export, backend, config, introspection.
@end menu

@node Global Options
@section Global options

@table @option
@item --json
Same as @samp{--format json}.

@item --format @var{format}
@cindex output formats
Output format: @code{json} (one document with @code{metadata} and
@code{data}), @code{jsonl} (one record per line), @code{yaml},
@code{csv}, or @code{explore}.  The default is a table on a terminal and
JSON otherwise.

@item --fields @var{field},@dots{}
Print only these fields of each record.

@item --dry-run
Show what would change, and whether the display server accepts it,
without changing anything.

@item -v
@itemx --verbose
Also print informational diagnostics, such as how the theme was chosen.

@item -q
@itemx --quiet
Print errors only.

@item --color @var{when}
@itemx --no-color
Colour human output @code{auto}, @code{always} or @code{never}.

@item -0
@itemx --print0
Print the first field of each record followed by a NUL byte.

@item -y
@itemx --yes
Keep changes without asking, overwrite existing files, and allow the
editor to open under an agent.

@item --backend @var{name}
Use this backend instead of detecting one (@pxref{Backends}).

@item -V
@itemx --version
Print the version, maintainer and project page.
@end table

@node Output Commands
@section Output commands

@table @code
@item vnr output list
@findex output list
List the connected outputs and their settings.

@item vnr output get @var{output}
@findex output get
Show one output, including every mode it supports.

@item vnr output set @var{output} @var{settings}@dots{}
@findex output set
Change one output.  A new mode, scale or transform changes the output's
size on the desktop, so Vernier moves the outputs beside it to keep their
edges touching: an output whose edge touched its right edge moves
sideways, as does anything lined up beside that one, and likewise below.
Outputs that did not touch it stay put; if two moved outputs would collide,
one is pushed clear.  @option{--pos} and the @option{--left-of}
family place the output explicitly and skip this.  The settings are:

@table @option
@item --on
@itemx --off
Turn the output on or off.
@item --mode @var{width}x@var{height}[@@@var{rate}]
Mode, rate in Hz, for example @samp{2560x1440@@144}.  A rate within
0.5 Hz of an advertised mode selects it.
@item --preferred
Use the display's preferred mode.
@item --pos @var{x},@var{y}
Absolute position in logical pixels.
@item --left-of @var{output}
@itemx --right-of @var{output}
@itemx --above @var{output}
@itemx --below @var{output}
Place next to another output.
@item --scale @var{factor}
Scale, at most three decimals, for example @samp{1.25}.
@item --transform @var{t}
One of @code{normal}, @code{90}, @code{180}, @code{270}, @code{flipped},
@code{flipped-90}, @code{flipped-180}, @code{flipped-270}.
@item --adaptive-sync @var{state}
@code{disabled}, @code{automatic} or @code{always}.
@item --mirror @var{output}
@itemx --no-mirror
Mirror another output, or stop mirroring.
@item --primary
Make this the primary output.
@item --timeout @var{seconds}
Seconds to wait for confirmation before reverting; 15 by default.
@item --persist @var{file}
Once the change is kept, also write niri @code{output} blocks for the
whole layout to @var{file} (@pxref{Making Changes Permanent}).
@item --save
On GNOME, have GNOME save the change for every login.  GNOME's own
dialog replaces Vernier's prompt, so @option{--save} cannot be combined
with @option{--yes}, @option{--timeout} or @option{--persist}
(@pxref{Making Changes Permanent}).
@end table
@end table

@example
vnr output set DP-1 --mode 2560x1440@@144 --right-of eDP-1 --scale 1.25
vnr output set HDMI-A-1 --mirror eDP-1 --dry-run --json
@end example

@node Other Commands
@section Other commands

@table @code
@item vnr profile save @var{file}
@findex profile save
Save the current layout as a profile (@pxref{Profiles}).

@item vnr profile apply @var{file}
@findex profile apply
Apply a saved profile, then keep or revert it.  Accepts
@option{--timeout}, @option{--persist} and @option{--save} as
@samp{output set} does.

@item vnr layout export --to @var{format}
@findex layout export
Print the layout as @code{niri}, @code{sway}, @code{kanshi},
@code{shikane} or @code{home-manager} configuration.
@option{--profile @var{file}} exports a profile instead of the current
layout; @option{--write @var{file}} writes a file instead of printing.

@item vnr backend get
@findex backend get
Show which backend is in use and what it can do.

@item vnr config check [@var{file}]
@findex config check
Validate the configuration file and list the key bindings in effect
(@pxref{Configuration File}).

@item vnr schema [@var{noun} [@var{verb}]]
@findex schema
Print the JSON Schema of every command, a noun, or one command, ready
for function calling by a language model.

@item vnr describe
@findex describe
Print the capability manifest: commands, flags, formats and exit codes.

@item vnr gui
@findex gui
Open the editor.  @option{--demo} uses a sample layout;
@option{--theme @var{slug}} and @option{--accessible} choose the theme.
@end table

@cindex allowed paths
Files are read and written only inside the working directory and
Vernier's own directories under @file{~/.config}, @file{~/.local/share}
and @file{~/.cache}; @samp{layout export --write} and
@option{--persist} may also write inside the niri, sway, kanshi,
shikane and home-manager configuration directories.  An existing file
is replaced only with @option{--yes}.

@node Profiles
@chapter Profiles

@cindex profiles
A profile is a TOML file with one @code{[[output]]} table per output.
@samp{vnr profile save} writes one; @samp{vnr profile apply} restores it.

@example
[[output]]
name = "DP-3"
make = "Dell Inc."
model = "U2720Q"
serial = "ABC123"
mode = "3840x2160@@60Hz"
position = @{ x = 1920, y = 0 @}
scale = 1.5
transform = "normal"
adaptive-sync = "automatic"

[[output]]
name = "eDP-1"
mirror = "DP-3"
@end example

@cindex matching displays
An entry that names make, model and serial matches that display on any
connector, so a profile survives a dock that renumbers its ports.
Fields left out keep the output's current value.  A key Vernier does
not know is an error that names the key, so a typo never silently drops
a setting.

@node Making Changes Permanent
@chapter Making Changes Permanent

@cindex persistence
@cindex niri
Changes made through wlr-output-management last until the compositor
restarts.  For niri, add @option{--persist} to a change:

@example
vnr output set eDP-1 --scale 1.5 --persist ~/.config/niri/vernier.kdl
@end example

Once you keep the change, Vernier writes niri @code{output} blocks for
the whole layout to that file.  Add this line to niri's
@file{config.kdl} once:

@example
include "vernier.kdl"
@end example

@option{--persist} never writes @file{config.kdl} itself, never follows a
symbolic link, and replaces a file only if Vernier generated it.  It
checks the file before anything on screen changes.

@cindex GNOME
On GNOME, Vernier's changes last until you log out.  To keep one for
every login, add @option{--save}:

@example
vnr output set eDP-1 --scale 1.5 --save
@end example

GNOME then asks on screen whether to keep the new layout, in place of
Vernier's prompt.  Choose @samp{Keep Changes} within 20 seconds and
GNOME saves it in @file{~/.config/monitors.xml}; otherwise GNOME
restores the previous layout and @command{vnr} exits with status 14
(@code{REVERTED}).  If the new layout stays up but Vernier sees no
sign that GNOME wrote the file, it keeps the change and warns
(@code{NOT_PERSISTENT}) rather than claim a save.  GNOME's dialog needs
someone at the screen, so
@option{--save} refuses @option{--yes}.  KDE Plasma saves every change
by itself, so @option{--save} changes nothing there; other backends
report it as unsupported.

@cindex kanshi
@cindex shikane
@cindex home-manager
For hotplug daemons, export a profile in their own format:

@example
vnr layout export --to kanshi --write ~/.config/kanshi/config
vnr layout export --to shikane
vnr layout export --to home-manager
@end example

The home-manager output is a @code{services.kanshi} setting to merge into
your home-manager configuration.

@node Configuration File
@chapter Configuration File

@cindex configuration file
@cindex Nickel
Vernier reads @file{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/vernier/config.ncl}
(@file{~/.config/vernier/config.ncl} by default), written in
@uref{https://nickel-lang.org/, Nickel}.  The file is optional; without
it every setting has its default.

@example
@{
  overlays = true,
  previews = false,
  keys = @{
    left = ["Left", "a"],
    right = ["Right", "d"],
    keep = ["y", "Enter"],
  @},
@}
@end example

@table @code
@item overlays
@code{true} (the default) shows screen labels while the editor is open;
@code{false} turns them off (@pxref{Screen Labels}).

@item previews
@code{true} starts the editor with each output's screen contents shown;
@code{false} (the default) starts it without them
(@pxref{Screen Contents}).

@item keys
A record from action names to lists of chords.  Each action listed
replaces its default bindings, an empty list unbinds it, and actions not
listed keep their defaults.  The action names are @code{apply},
@code{reload}, @code{discard}, @code{keep}, @code{revert}, @code{next},
@code{previous}, @code{left}, @code{right}, @code{up}, @code{down} and
@code{previews} (@pxref{Editor Keys}).
@end table

@cindex chords
A chord is a key, optionally preceded by @samp{Ctrl+} and @samp{Alt+}:
@samp{Ctrl+Enter}, @samp{Alt+F2}, @samp{PageDown}, @samp{]} or
@samp{h}.  Named keys are @code{Enter}, @code{Escape}, @code{Space},
@code{Backspace}, @code{Delete}, @code{Insert}, @code{Home}, @code{End},
@code{PageUp}, @code{PageDown}, @code{Left}, @code{Right}, @code{Up},
@code{Down} and @code{F1} to @code{F24}.  @key{Shift} cannot be bound,
because it makes moves ten times larger; @key{TAB} cannot be bound,
because it moves keyboard focus.  One chord may not belong to two
actions.

@findex config check
Run @samp{vnr config check} to validate the file.  Every problem is
listed, with the field and line it concerns.  If the file has a problem
when the editor starts, the editor still opens, with the default key
bindings, and says why in its status line.

@node Backends
@chapter Backends

@cindex backends
Vernier picks a backend from the session.  @option{--backend} or
@env{VNR_BACKEND} overrides it.

@multitable @columnfractions .14 .40 .46
@headitem Name @tab Sessions @tab Protocol
@item @code{wlr}
@tab niri, sway, Hyprland, river, labwc, Wayfire
@tab wlr-output-management, versions 1 to 4
@item @code{cosmic}
@tab COSMIC
@tab wlr-output-management with COSMIC's extension, which adds
mirroring, fine scale and the Xwayland primary output
@item @code{kde}
@tab KDE Plasma 6
@tab kde-output-management-v2
@item @code{mutter}
@tab GNOME
@tab @code{org.gnome.Mutter.DisplayConfig} over D-Bus
@item @code{x11}
@tab X11 sessions
@tab RandR 1.5
@end multitable

@cindex capabilities
@samp{vnr backend get} shows what the backend in use can do: mirroring,
adaptive sync, a primary output, custom modes, fractional scale, testing
before applying, whether the display server keeps changes across a
restart by itself, and whether it saves one on request
(@option{--save}).

@node Environment
@chapter Environment

@cindex environment variables
@table @env
@item VNR_BACKEND
Backend to use instead of detecting one.

@item VNR_ALLOWED_PATHS
Extra directories, separated as in @env{PATH}, that Vernier may read and
write.

@item VNR_FIXTURE
A JSON file to use as a fake display server, for tests.

@item XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base directory of @file{vernier/config.ncl} and of the allowed write
locations; @file{~/.config} when unset.

@item SPACECRAFT_THEME
Theme slug, for example @code{steelbore} or
@code{steelbore-navywhite}.  @option{--theme} overrides it.

@item SPACECRAFT_A11Y
@code{1} turns accessible mode on (high-contrast theme, no animation);
@code{0} turns it off.

@item NO_COLOR
@itemx FORCE_COLOR
Turn colour off, or force it on, as usual.

@item AI_AGENT
@itemx AGENT
@itemx CI
@itemx CLAUDECODE
When any of these is set, Vernier prints JSON and never prompts.
@end table

@node Exit Status
@chapter Exit Status

@cindex exit status
@cindex errors
Errors are printed to standard error as one JSON object with a
@code{code}, a @code{message} and a @code{hint} that is itself a command
you can run.

@multitable @columnfractions .10 .30 .60
@headitem Status @tab Code @tab Meaning
@item 0 @tab @tab Success.
@item 1 @tab @code{INTERNAL_ERROR} @tab An unexpected failure inside Vernier.
@item 2 @tab @code{INVALID_ARGUMENT}, @code{MISSING_ARGUMENT} @tab An argument is wrong or missing.
@item 3 @tab @code{NOT_FOUND} @tab The output, profile or file does not exist.
@item 4 @tab @code{PERMISSION_DENIED} @tab A path is outside the allowed set, or access was refused.
@item 5 @tab @code{CONFLICT} @tab The file already exists, or the outputs changed while applying.
@item 10 @tab @code{BACKEND_UNAVAILABLE} @tab No display server, or it lacks the protocol.
@item 11 @tab @code{UNSUPPORTED} @tab The backend cannot express the change.
@item 12 @tab @code{REJECTED} @tab The display server refused the configuration.
@item 13 @tab @code{PROTOCOL_ERROR} @tab The display server sent something unexpected.
@item 14 @tab @code{REVERTED} @tab The change was not confirmed and was undone.
@item 15 @tab @code{INVALID_LAYOUT} @tab The layout has errors and was not sent.
@item 16 @tab @code{REVERT_FAILED} @tab Restoring the previous configuration failed.
@item 17 @tab @code{LIBRARY_MISSING} @tab The editor cannot load a library it needs.
@end multitable

@node Reporting Bugs
@chapter Reporting Bugs

@cindex bugs
Report problems at
@uref{https://github.com/Spacecraft-Software/Vernier/issues}.  Include
the output of @samp{vnr --verbose backend get} and the command you ran.
Security problems go privately to the maintainer, Mohamed Hammad,
at @email{Mohamed.Hammad@@SpacecraftSoftware.org}, as described in the
project's @file{SECURITY.md}.

